WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.115
As a server author: validate, limit, sanitize.

2
00:00:03.395 --> 00:00:14.019
The tools page lists what servers must do: validate all tool inputs, implement proper access controls, rate limit tool invocations, and sanitize tool outputs.

3
00:00:14.299 --> 00:00:17.051
Each has a concrete meaning for the tracker you built.

4
00:00:17.331 --> 00:00:19.357
Validate inputs beyond the schema.

5
00:00:19.637 --> 00:00:33.845
The SDK rejects wrong types before your function runs, but only your code knows that HAR-101 must match a pattern, that done can only move back to open, and that a limit of ten thousand is a mistake.

6
00:00:34.125 --> 00:00:40.589
Be strictest where a string becomes something else: a file path, a URL, a shell argument, or a query.

7
00:00:40.869 --> 00:00:51.237
Willison’s April 2025 survey of MCP problems opens with exactly that bug class, servers passing unescaped strings to os.

8
00:00:51.517 --> 00:00:52.349
system().

9
00:00:52.629 --> 00:01:05.813
The v2 Python SDK’s MCPServer adds one guard by default: its resource security settings reject path traversal, absolute paths, and null bytes in resource template parameters.

10
00:01:06.093 --> 00:01:08.269
Tool arguments are still yours to check.

11
00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:09.851
Sanitize outputs.

12
00:01:10.131 --> 00:01:14.611
What your tool returns enters the model’s context and often a person’s screen.

13
00:01:14.891 --> 00:01:22.293
The lab’s clean_text removes invisible Unicode format characters and control characters, and truncates long text.

14
00:01:22.573 --> 00:01:27.693
Those characters matter because they can make what the model reads differ from what a reviewer sees.

15
00:01:27.973 --> 00:01:31.899
Cleaning does not make text trustworthy; it makes the deception visible.

16
00:01:32.179 --> 00:01:33.907
Return little, and label it.

17
00:01:34.187 --> 00:01:39.520
Summaries by default, full text on request, and a note that user-written fields are data.

18
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:50.339
Claude Code warns when a tool result passes 10,000 tokens, and large results are both expensive and a larger surface for injected text.

19
00:01:50.619 --> 00:01:51.749
Fail safely.

20
00:01:52.029 --> 00:02:00.029
Unexpected exceptions should reach the model as a generic failure while the traceback stays in your log, which the SDK does for you.

21
00:02:00.309 --> 00:02:10.507
Then test all of it adversarially, without a model: bad ids, path traversal strings, oversized inputs, injection text in the data, and repeated writes.

22
00:02:10.787 --> 00:02:15.459
Those tests are cheap, deterministic, and the only way to know your checks hold.
